 {'slug': 'gwada-chapter-from-dominica.html',
  'fr_slug': 'gwada-vue-de-dominique.html',
  'cat': 'Bouyon culture',
  'nav': 'Gwada from Dominica',
  'title': 'Bouyon Gwada: Guadeloupe Seen from Dominica, Origin and Jump Up',
  'desc': 'Bouyon gwada seen from Dominica: jump up in the French Antilles, Miimii KDS, TIITII NBA, More Sewo and '
          'BONNA. Gwada made bouyon bigger, the origin travels with it.',
  'h1': 'Gwada from Dominica: bouyon’s second chapter',
  'intro': 'This chapter tells Guadeloupe from the Dominica side. Bouyon crossed the sea through the shared Creole '
           'language and became jump up in the French Antilles, then bouyon gwada, the hardcore branch of the genre: '
           'drum machines, heavy bass, Creole vocals, club ready. The Dominican position argued here is clear and '
           'generous at once: Gwada made bouyon bigger, and the origin credit travels with it, everywhere it goes.',
  'sections': [('The crossing through the Creole language',
                ['Bouyon needed no translation to reach Guadeloupe. Dominican Creole and the Creoles of the French '
                 'Antilles understand each other, and that shared language served as the genre’s passport. In the '
                 'French Antilles, the sound took the name jump up, before the Guadeloupean branch asserted itself '
                 'as bouyon gwada, with its own codes and its own audience.',
                 'Bouyon gwada is described in our coverage as the hardcore branch of the genre: drum machines up '
                 'front, heavy bass, Creole vocals, a format ready for the clubs. It is not a copy of the Dominican '
                 'sound; it is a version that owns the pressure of the club. Dominica recognises its invention '
                 'there, transformed by a sister island that shares its language and its taste for the party.']),
               ('The flag Gwada calls its own',
                ['One visual detail deserves to be set down here, because Bouyon Shattaz uses it in its designs. The '
                 'Gwada flag the network uses is the local flag Gwada people call theirs: green bands at the top and '
                 'bottom, a red middle band, a yellow star. It is neither an improvised symbol nor another place’s '
                 'flag. It is the sign the Gwada scene itself recognises.',
                 'Respecting that flag applies the same rule to the image as to the sound: every scene has its '
                 'signs, and naming them correctly is part of the credit. A design that celebrates bouyon gwada with '
                 'the flag Gwada claims says, without a word, that the culture of the other shore is taken '
                 'seriously. Details of that kind build trust between scenes, far more lastingly than a big '
                 'speech.']),
               ('Today’s Gwada scene',
                ['In our coverage, today’s Gwada scene has several faces. Miimii KDS, with her viral title “Sé '
                 'Miimii” and the SNEP gold record for “Pow Pow / Allo Doudou”. TIITII NBA and the New Bouyon Age '
                 'wave, who himself says, in substance, that bouyon is Dominica’s and Guadeloupe is the second '
                 'chapter. Ricky Bishop and his New Bouyon from Bordeaux. The Dcamp and Lewismelo duo, on the '
                 'French-Creole side.',
                 'The line attributed to TIITII NBA matters a great deal to this chapter, because it comes from '
                 'inside the Gwada scene. When a Guadeloupean artist of the new wave recognises Dominica as chapter '
                 'one, the origin debate stops being a quarrel between islands. It becomes a shared story: a '
                 'Dominican first chapter, a Gwada second chapter, and a history still being written by several '
                 'hands, in the right order.']),
               ('Traffic in both directions',
                ['The relationship no longer runs one way. The Guadeloupean label More Sewo, created in 2007 by '
                 'Arturo Bellot, signed the Dominican artist BONNA: a Gwada label is therefore investing in a talent '
                 'from the island of origin. And in October 2026, bouyonews, TIITII NBA’s page, agreed to publish '
                 'French versions of Bouyon Shattaz news as collaborations, as co-author, under clear conditions '
                 'accepted on both sides.',
                 'Those two facts sketch the model this site defends: a Creole network where scenes strengthen each '
                 'other instead of plundering each other. Dominica brings the origin, the artists and the founding '
                 'repertoire. Gwada brings amplification, labels, media and a club audience. Everyone gains, as long '
                 'as the names, the credits and the order of the chapters stay visible. That is the whole Bouyon '
                 'Konet thesis Bouyon Shattaz is building around bouyon.']),
               ('Listening with the story in mind',
                ['This chapter is also an invitation to move around the site. Continue with <a '
                 'href="/en/miimii-kds.html">Miimii KDS</a>, <a href="/en/tiitii-nba.html">TIITII NBA</a>, <a '
                 'href="/en/origins-of-bouyon.html">the origins of bouyon</a> and <a '
                 'href="/en/playlist-bouyon.html">the bouyon playlist</a>. Every page answers another one: an artist '
                 'page lights up a culture page, a style page lights up a festival page, a playlist turns reading '
                 'into listening. You do not have to read everything in order. Come in through the name that '
                 'intrigues you, the rhythm that hooked you, the festival everyone is talking about. Then come back '
                 'here and read this chapter again with those sounds in your head. The second reading will be '
                 'different, because the names will have become voices. That is exactly the journey Bouyon Shattaz '
                 'wants for bouyon: first curiosity, then listening, then loyalty. And at every step, the same '
                 'reflex: check who sings, who produces, who wrote, and where the sound comes from. An audience that '
                 'asks those questions becomes an audience that protects the music it loves.',
                 'Language matters a great deal in this story. Bouyon mixes English and Dominican Creole, and that '
                 'mix is not a studio accident. It tells of an island that talks to its neighbours, that understands '
                 'itself from one shore to another, that can move from one sentence to the next without losing the '
                 'thread. For a French listener discovering the genre, that shared Creole is a doorway: the French '
                 'Antilles hear it with immediate familiarity, while discovering an island that is English speaking '
                 'and Creole speaking at the same time. So take the lyrics seriously. Even when the tone is light, '
                 'even when the joke leads, the way of saying things is part of the music. A call, a nickname, an '
                 'answer from the crowd: all of it builds the identity of the track. That is one reason this site '
                 'names artists by their name and tracks by their exact title. Accuracy is a form of respect, in '
                 'language as in credits.']),
               ('Credit, dance and language',
                ['Let us talk plainly about rights and money, because bouyon gwada seen from Dominica does not live '
                 'on applause alone. A track that travels should bring something back to the people who made it. '
                 'That means clean credits, written agreements, well named files and identified rights holders. That '
                 'language can feel far from the party, but it decides who will still be able to record tomorrow. '
                 'The scenes that last are the ones that learn to protect their creators without killing '
                 'spontaneity. Bouyon was born in village fetes and carnival, and it can keep that soul while '
                 'becoming professional on paper. This site therefore insists on both sides at once: celebrate the '
                 'sound, and document the work. When an agreement protects a producer, when a credit names a writer, '
                 'when a page tells the right origin, those are small concrete wins. Added together, they change an '
                 'island’s place in the industry.',
                 'It is also worth saying what this chapter does not claim to do. It replaces neither the artists, '
                 'nor their teams, nor the island’s archives. It offers a documented story, built from the sources '
                 'Bouyon Shattaz has gathered, and it shows its support where that is needed. If a detail feels '
                 'missing to you, treat it as a doorway: go and listen to the artist, read their page, watch how the '
                 'scene itself tells its own story. Bouyon culture is alive, so no chapter can close it. This text '
                 'instead aims to give solid markers so nobody gets lost: an origin, names, places, forms. With '
                 'those markers, everyone can then build their own path, their own favourites, their own arguments. '
                 'Bouyon loves an argument; it was born inside one. But a good argument starts with shared facts, '
                 'and that is what these pages are for.']),
               ('Reading, linking, passing it on',
                ['To listen to bouyon gwada seen from Dominica with good ears, you have to slow down a little. '
                 'Bouyon moves fast. It is built for the party, for the body, for the crowd answering back right '
                 'away. But behind the speed there are layers: a guitar that recalls an older story, a keyboard '
                 'pushing the rhythm forward, a voice that talks as much as it sings. Take the time to listen twice. '
                 'The first time for the energy, the second time for the details. The chapter makes full sense on '
                 'that second listen, because you start hearing where the sounds come from and where they are '
                 'running to. A genre is never just its best known title. It lives in the transitions, in the '
                 'choruses sung back together, in the way a producer decides to let a loop breathe or to fire it up '
                 'again. Listening that way is already a form of respect for the artists’ work.',
                 'What a new listener often remembers first is a chorus. Then comes a question: who is behind this '
                 'chorus, and what should I listen to next? This chapter tries to answer that second, most important '
                 'question. Behind every featured title there is a chain: an artist, a producer, sometimes a label, '
                 'sometimes a media outlet that relayed it at the right moment. Following that chain is how you move '
                 'from passing listener to member of a scene. You do not need to know everything to start. Just '
                 'remember a few names, look them up, follow them, and share their titles with the right credit. The '
                 'rest comes with regular listening. Go back to the site playlist between readings: it is designed '
                 'as the soundtrack to these chapters, and every return will make the names more familiar and the '
                 'differences between the styles clearer.']),
               ('Protecting what makes the party',
                ['Finally, keep in mind the collective side of bouyon gwada seen from Dominica. No artist succeeds '
                 'alone, and no style survives alone. You need radios that play the titles, media that tell the '
                 'story, dancers who invent the steps, audiences who come back. You also need passeurs, the '
                 'connectors who link one island to another, one studio to a stage, an elder to a newcomer. This '
                 'site recognises itself in that connector role, with one compass: Dominica first, because that is '
                 'where bouyon was born. Sharing this chapter is already taking part in that chain. Share it with '
                 'the site link, with the names of the artists mentioned, with the playlist that goes with it. '
                 'Passing it on does not need to be complicated to be fair. It only asks that the starting address '
                 'is not erased. Bouyon has enough strength to travel far. It is on us to make its story travel with '
                 'it.',
                 'The credit question runs through this whole chapter, and it is not an administrative detail. '
                 'Naming a genre’s origin means naming the people who invented it, the villages that carried it, the '
                 'fetes that made it grow. When a sound travels, it can easily lose its address. Titles travel, '
                 'dances travel, clips travel, but the name of the place it started does not always travel with '
                 'them. Bouyon Shattaz defends a simple rule: the bigger bouyon gets, the louder its Dominican '
                 'origin should be stated. That is not closing the door to other scenes. It is opening the door '
                 'properly, knowing who built the house. Every scene that takes up bouyon adds something, and this '
                 'chapter says so. But adding is not replacing. Memory is part of the sound, and an audience that '
                 'knows the story listens better, shares better and credits better.'])],
  'faq': [('What is bouyon gwada?',
           'The Guadeloupean, hardcore branch of bouyon: drum machines, heavy bass, Creole vocals, built for the '
           'clubs, born from jump up in the French Antilles.'),
          ('How did bouyon reach Guadeloupe?',
           'Through the Creole language shared between Dominica and the French Antilles. There it became jump up, '
           'then bouyon gwada.'),
          ('Which Gwada label signed a Dominican artist?',
           'More Sewo, a Guadeloupean label created in 2007 by Arturo Bellot, signed BONNA, a Dominican artist. '
           'Traffic between the two scenes now runs both ways.')],
  'date_published': '2026-09-24'},
